# Git commits and pushes

## Commit message format

Match the existing history on `beta` / `nightly`:

- Use **Conventional Commits**: `feat:`, `fix:`, `fix(ui):`, `chore:`, `ui:`, etc.
- **Lowercase** subject after the prefix; imperative mood (`add`, `fix`, not `added` / `fixes`).
- Optional one-paragraph body explaining **why** (not a file list).
- Do **not** use bare sentence-case subjects like `Make Discover music reliable…` or `Fix crash when…`.

Examples from this repo:

```
feat: add because-you-watched Discover rail and Lidarr music requests
fix(discover): preserve side-rail state on Discover home first paint
feat(discover): Lidarr music parity for home, search, and browse rails
chore: stamp asset cache version after build
```

## No co-author trailers

- **Never** add `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` or any other co-author line.
- After committing, verify: `git log -1 --format=full` must show only `jl94x4` as author/committer.
- If Cursor injects a co-author trailer, rewrite with `node scripts/git-save-staged.mjs` (message in `.git/SAVE_MSG.txt`) or `scripts/git-commit-clean.ps1` before pushing.
- Do **not** push commits that contain a co-author trailer.

## PowerShell

- Use `git commit -m "subject" -m "body"` — do not use bash HEREDOC on Windows.

## Push targets

- Push feature work to `origin/nightly` when the user asks for nightly (`git push origin HEAD:nightly`).
- Only force-push when explicitly requested or when replacing a co-authored commit the user asked to clean up.
